    <div class="research-block">
      <h3>Analog Quantum Simulation of Lattice Gauge Theories</h3>
      <p>
        Understanding how matter behaves and interacts at the most basic level requires simulating 
        quantum field theories, which are the physical framework behind electromagnetism, the strong force, 
        and much more physics. However, even the most powerful classical computers struggle to simulate this physics, 
        especially for far-out-of-equilibrium physics. In my most recent work, in collaboration with the <a href = "https://www.phys.ethz.ch/the-department/people/person-detail.marinama.html">Marinkovic group</a> 
        at ETH Zürich, we propose using arrays of optical or microwave cavities, 
        where photons and atomic systems form hybrid quantum particles called polaritons, to serve as an analog quantum 
        simulator of these theories. By engineering specific resonance conditions between the polaritonic states, 
        the proposed quantum simulator would naturally obey the same mathematical rules as the target field theory, 
        with no additional constraints needed. This approach is compatible with existing state-of-the-art photonic and 
        superconducting quantum hardware, offering realistic near term quantum simulations of beyond-classically 
        accessible physics. In the long run, quantum simulators such as these could answer some of the biggest 
        unanswered questions in particle physics and beyond.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="research-block">
      <h3>Nanophotonic Devices with Color Centers</h3>
      <p>
        Future quantum technologies will need to be able to communicate over a so called <em>Quantum Internet</em>.
        Color centers, which are atomic defects in the lattice of semiconductors, form localized electronic states
        which can be used to store and communicate quantum information. Much of my research aims to integrate color centers
        into nanophotonic devices to enhance their propreties for use as quantum memories or repeaters.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="research-block">
      <h3>Trapped Ion Quantum Computing</h3>
      <p>
        One of the major challenges of trapped-ion quantum computation lies in the complexity of the optical systems
        used to cool and manipulate the ions. This work, done in collaboration with UC Berkeley, Santa Barbara, and Irvine
        aims to miniturize the optical components required for trapped ion quantum computation onto a scalable, 
        chip-based integrated photonic platform.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

